## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design privacy-preserving retention controls at the device edge, balancing storage, CPU, and regulatory costs while quantifying the business impact of data minimization.

## Key Concepts

- On-device policy engines and interpreters
- Data minimization and retention windows
- Batch policy delivery and anonymized audit deltas
- Compliance costs and telemetry design

## Code Example

```javascript
// Policy evaluator
function shouldDelete(event, policy) {
  const rule = policy.findRule(event.type);
  return event.timestamp < Date.now() - policy.getRetentionPeriod(rule);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Gauges practical CBA skills for privacy-conscious features that touch user data, emphasizing cost modeling, data minimization, and risk assessment.

## Key Concepts
- On-device processing, data minimization, and opt-in consent
- Batch sizing, compression, and secure transmission
- Cost levers: storage, CPU energy, network, compliance, and hosting
- Go/no-go criteria tied to break-even and risk tolerance

## Code Example
```javascript
function batchEvents(events, batchSize) {
  const batches = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < events.length; i += batchSize) {
    batches.push(events.slice(i, i + batchSize));
  }
  return batches;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate privacy risk post-implementation (DPIA outcomes, audits)?
- What metrics signal a need to adjust batch size or cadence?

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores a privacy-preserving on-device machine learning use case with real-world constraints including energy efficiency, latency requirements, data minimization principles, and regulatory compliance. It emphasizes the importance of measurable go/no-go criteria and a concrete break-even timeline for implementation decisions.

## Key Concepts
- On-device machine learning models (autoencoder/isolation forest)
- Energy and performance budgeting for background tasks
- Data minimization and privacy impact assessments
- Go/No-Go criteria and break-even horizon analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function detectAnomaly(readings, threshold){
  const mean = readings.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/readings.length;
  const variance = readings.reduce((a,b)=>a+Math.pow(b-mean,2),0)/readings.length;
  return Math.abs(readings[readings.length-1] - mean) > threshold * Math.sqrt(variance);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges ability to justify product bets with a pragmatic, numbers-driven approach and to handle uncertainty in inputs.

## Key Concepts

- Cost estimation
- Benefit estimation
- Break-even analysis
- Reasonable assumptions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple CBA calculator
function cba(params) {
  const {maU, lift, arpu, months, cost} = params;
  const annualizedBenefit = maU * lift * arpu * months;
  return {annualizedBenefit, cost, breakevenMonths: cost / (annualizedBenefit || 1)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust if MAU grows or adoption changes?
- What if ARPU uplift is not uniform across user segments?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design a cost-aware, privacy-preserving XAI feature with on-device constraints and compliance.

## Key Concepts

- On-device explainability budgets, latency targets, and data locality
- Telemetry storage limits, retention, and privacy controls
- Cost trade-offs between local inference vs cloud-audit

## Code Example

```javascript
function capLogs(logs, maxBytes) {
  // Simplified capping by character length
  let acc = '';
  for (const s of logs) {
    if ((acc + s).length <= maxBytes) acc += s;
    else break;
  }
  return acc;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle model drift and auditability across OS updates?
- What metrics would you track for go/no-go decisions and how would you set thresholds?

